# Git Workflow - Multi-Repo Structure

## Repository Structure
```
Resmo/                    # Workspace (has own git repo)
├── .cursor/rules/        # Shared rules (committed here)
├── package.json          # Workspace config
└── apps/                 # IGNORED in workspace repo
    ├── backend/          # Separate git repo
    ├── superadmin/       # Separate git repo
    ├── company/          # Separate git repo
    ├── admin/            # Separate git repo
    └── conseiller/       # Separate git repo
```

## Committing Changes

### App Changes (backend, superadmin, etc.)
```bash
# Navigate to the specific app
cd apps/superadmin

# Stage and commit
git add .
git commit -m "feat(superadmin): add company category field"
git push origin main
```

### Workspace Changes (rules, root config)
```bash
# From workspace root
cd Resmo

# Stage and commit
git add .cursor/rules/ package.json
git commit -m "chore: update cursor rules"
git push origin main
```

## Which Repo to Commit To?

| Changed Files | Commit To |
|--------------|-----------|
| `apps/backend/*` | `apps/backend` repo |
| `apps/superadmin/*` | `apps/superadmin` repo |
| `apps/company/*` | `apps/company` repo |
| `.cursor/rules/*` | Workspace root repo |
| `package.json` (root) | Workspace root repo |

## Cross-App Features
When a feature spans multiple apps:
1. Commit backend changes first → push
2. Commit frontend changes → push
3. Use same commit message prefix for traceability

```bash
# Backend
cd apps/backend
git commit -m "feat(api): add business category endpoint"

# Frontend  
cd apps/superadmin
git commit -m "feat(superadmin): add business category selector"
```

## NEVER commit from workspace root expecting app changes to be included.
